EBS Volume Management and EC2 Scaling in AWS

========================================================
========================================================

========================================================
Create Volume → Attach → lsblk → fdisk → mkfs → mkdir → mount → fstab → reboot
========================================
AWS EBS VOLUME ATTACHMENT AND PERSISTENCE

STEP 4 : VERIFY ATTACHED DISK
Command:
lsblk

Expected Output:

Root Volume : nvme0n1
New EBS Disk : nvme1n1

STEP 5 : CREATE PARTITION
Command:
sudo fdisk /dev/nvme1n1

Inside fdisk enter:

n
p
1

w

Explanation:
n -> Create new partition
p -> Primary partition
1 -> Partition number
w -> Save changes


STEP 6 : VERIFY PARTITION
Command:
lsblk

Expected Output:
nvme1n1
└─nvme1n1p1


STEP 7 : FORMAT PARTITION WITH XFS
Command:
sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme1n1p1

Verify Filesystem:
lsblk -fs

Expected:
nvme1n1p1 xfs


STEP 8 : CREATE MOUNT DIRECTORY
Command:
sudo mkdir /mnt/cseb


STEP 9 : MOUNT THE VOLUME
Command:
sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/cseb

Verify Mount:
df -h

Expected:
Filesystem Size Used Avail Mounted on
/dev/nvme1n1p1 100G ... ... /mnt/cseb


STEP 10 : MAKE MOUNT PERSISTENT
Get UUID:
sudo blkid /dev/nvme1n1p1

Example Output:
/dev/nvme1n1p1: UUID="e14d4020-d4f3-4f88-bd4c-89aa97aca41a" TYPE="xfs"

Edit fstab:
sudo nano /etc/fstab

Add this line at the end:

UUID=e14d4020-d4f3-4f88-bd4c-89aa97aca41a /mnt/cseb xfs defaults,nofail 0 0

Save and Exit:
CTRL + O
ENTER
CTRL + X

Test fstab:
sudo mount -a

If no error appears -> configuration is correct.

Verify:
df -h


STEP 11 : CREATE FILE IN EBS VOLUME
Go to mounted directory:
cd /mnt/cseb

Create file:
sudo touch testfile.txt

Add content:
echo "EBS Volume Persistence Test" | sudo tee testfile.txt

Verify:
cat testfile.txt

Expected Output:
EBS Volume Persistence Test


STEP 12 : UNMOUNT BEFORE DETACH
Command:
sudo umount /mnt/cseb

Verify:
df -h


DETACH VOLUME FROM AWS CONSOLE
Open EC2 Dashboard
Click Volumes
Select EBS Volume
Actions -> Detach Volume
Wait until state becomes:
available

ATTACH VOLUME TO ANOTHER EC2 INSTANCE
Select same EBS Volume
Actions -> Attach Volume
Select another EC2 instance
Device name:
/dev/sdf
Wait until state becomes:
in-use

ON SECOND EC2 INSTANCE
Check attached disk:
lsblk

Expected:
nvme1n1
└─nvme1n1p1


CREATE NEW MOUNT DIRECTORY
Command:
sudo mkdir /mnt/archana


MOUNT EXISTING EBS VOLUME
Command:
sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/archana


VERIFY DATA PERSISTENCE
Go to directory:
cd /mnt/archana

List files:
ls

Expected:
testfile.txt

Read file:
cat testfile.txt

Expected Output:
EBS Volume Persistence Test
